.TH "tum\-push" "1" "" "" "tmux\-borrow Manual"
.SS NAME
\f[B]tum\-push\f[R] \[em] detach a borrowed pane onto the LIFO stack
.SS SYNOPSIS
\f[B]tum push\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]tum push\f[R] \f[B]\-h\f[R] | \f[B]\[en]help\f[R]
.SS DESCRIPTION
Detach the focused borrowed pane onto a LIFO stack.
The pane parks in the hidden session with no host; its geometry is
recorded and a push sequence number is stamped on its state.
Push is how you hand a utility to another session: push here, \f[B]tum
pop\f[R] there.
.PP
Push is context\-aware like \f[B]tum flip\f[R] and \f[B]tum close\f[R]:
from a focused borrowed pane it pushes that pane; from a host with one
borrowed thing it pushes that; from a host with several it opens the
chooser.
.PP
Push is independent of the pool/owned distinction \[em] \f[CR]home\f[R]
is untouched, and a pushed pane attaches to whoever pops it.
A pushed pane has no host and is \f[B]not\f[R] reachable by \f[B]tum
flip\f[R]; bring it back only with \f[B]tum pop\f[R].
Non\-contiguous sequence numbers are fine, since panes may be closed
between pushes.
.SS OPTIONS
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\[en]help\f[R] Print a short synopsis (the SYNOPSIS
and EXAMPLES sections of this page) and exit.
For bare \f[B]tum\f[R], list every subcommand.
.SS EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
tum push                 # focused borrowed pane (or host\[aq]s single) onto the stack
# bound to  t p  in the shipped tum\-bindings.conf
# then in another session:  tum pop
.EE
